 "answer": {
  "answer": "The study confirmed 3 of the 5 cases it was asked for: 023b7fcc at 0 s shift, 0.75x speed; 12855a41 at 0 s, 0.75x; and 19f339ba at \u22120.5 s, 1.25x. All three were at 200 ms planner delay, and the ego failed in every repeat at each. None of these failures was the policy rear-ending the lead car. In every failed run the policy drifted about 1\u20132 m left of the recorded path, often against a RIGHT route command, and hit a car in the lane to its left. On 19f339ba it also braked for no clear reason while the lead was about 50 m ahead. So the study found no timing or speed of the lead car that makes VaVAM rear-end it. What it found is a repeatable leftward drift into the next lane under 200 ms delay. With only three runs per confirmed setting, each failure rate is only shown to be above about one half, and the study cannot say how much the retiming itself causes the drift.",
  "findings": [
   {
    "claim": "On 023b7fcc, with the lead car slowed to 0.75x at 200 ms delay, every repeat failed. In each, the policy moved into the left lane against a RIGHT command and side-swiped actor 56. It was not a rear-end. In the one run each at 1.0x and 1.25x, the ego did not fail.",
    "settings": [
